.TH NOSCAT 1 "2026" "noscat 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
noscat \- like netcat, but over a Nostr-native mesh: npub-addressed tunnels, no root, no accounts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B noscat
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ] " " [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B noscat
gives every machine a self-generated Nostr identity (a keypair - no email, no
phone number, no account), and tunnels encrypted traffic between machines by
that identity. The data plane is direct end-to-end encryption over UDP with
NAT hole punching; Nostr relays are only ever the discovery/signaling channel.
The wire protocol is specified in the draft NIP shipped in the repository
(docs/nip-noscat.md).
.PP
Every data-plane command shares one node identity per machine: the first run
generates it and stores it at
.I ~/.noscat/identity.hex
(mode 0600), and later runs reuse it, so your npub is stable. Pass
.B \-\-identity
to override. Commands that listen print the npub and a scannable pairing QR
(the QR encodes
.IR noscat:<npub> )
as soon as they are ready.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B keygen
Generate a fresh ephemeral node identity. With
.B \-\-show\-secret
also prints the hex secret (nsec). Rarely needed: the shared identity is
created automatically on first use.
.TP
.B advertise
Publish an overlay advert to relays so peers can discover this node.
.B \-\-endpoint <transport>/<ip:port>
(repeatable) names where you are reachable;
.B \-\-ttl <secs>
is the advert lifetime (default 3600).
.TP
.B watch
Watch relays for noscat adverts from any node.
.TP
.B serve
Serve a tunnel: advertise a UDP endpoint and bridge the first peer to stdio.
Prints the npub and pairing QR immediately; Ctrl-C quits.
.B \-\-listen <addr>
(default 0.0.0.0:41641).
.TP
.B up
The persistent mesh node: prints its npub and pairing QR, accepts any number
of inbound peers, saves inbound files into
.B \-\-out
(default: ~/Downloads), bridges raw inbound tunnels to
.B \-\-to <addr>
(when given), and runs a terminal chat. Requires
.B \-\-mesh:
membership is the authorization for inbound connections. The mesh owner also
answers join requests: devices publishing key packages are shown, and each is
invited once confirmed with
.BR y .
Answers other than y (including EOF) decline. Runs until Ctrl-C.
.TP
.BI pipe " target"
Pipe stdio into a tunnel to a peer by npub. Local stdin EOF sends a FIN; the
session closes when both sides are done.
.TP
.BI send " path target"
Send a file over a tunnel to a peer by npub. Transfers are resumable: an
interrupted attempt leaves a partial file plus a sidecar on the receiver, and
the next send of the same file picks up where the last one stopped. The
finished file is verified against the sender's sha256 before it is renamed
into place.
.TP
.B receive
Receive one file over a tunnel into
.B \-\-out
(default: the current directory). Prints the npub and pairing QR immediately.
.TP
.BI "expose \-\-to " addr
Expose a local TCP service to mesh peers over tunnels: each tunnel is
spliced to a fresh connection to the target address. Serves concurrent
sessions from one shared UDP port. Runs until interrupted.
.TP
.BI forward " target"
Forward a local TCP port over tunnels to a peer by npub (the side running
.BR expose ).
The listener binds 127.0.0.1 by default; pass
.B \-\-bind <ip>
to expose it further - anyone who can reach it can make this process dial the
target. Each connection dials its own tunnel.
.SH MESHES
A mesh is one MLS group per owner's set of devices (OpenMLS). Membership is
authorization: the group's epoch secret becomes the tunnel PSK, so scoped
tunnels only complete for current members. Removing a device ratchets the
epoch and locks it out immediately.
.PP
The device directory holds the device identity and MLS state and defaults to
.IR ~/.noscat/device ;
pass
.B \-\-dir <path>
to keep several devices on one machine.
.TP
.B mesh create
Create a mesh owned by this device's directory (once, on the owner).
.TP
.B mesh publish [\-\-owner <npub>]
Publish this device's MLS key package as a join request. With
.BR \-\-owner ,
the request is addressed to that owner alone; with
.BR \-\-mesh ,
the device also watches for the resulting invitation.
.TP
.B mesh invite <npub>
Invite a device into this (owner's) mesh.
.TP
.B mesh join [\-\-owner <npub>]
Wait for a gift-wrapped invitation and join the mesh; with
.BR \-\-owner ,
invitations from anyone else are ignored.
.TP
.B mesh members
List the mesh's members and epoch.
.TP
.B mesh remove <npub>
Remove a device from this (owner's) mesh; the epoch ratchets.
.TP
.B mesh sync
Merge pending mesh commits addressed to this device (run after an owner
invited or removed someone).
.TP
.B mesh psk
Print the mesh's current epoch tunnel PSK (hex).
.SH CHAT
The
.B up
node runs a terminal chat on stdin: type
.B to
to list the mesh's devices,
.BI to " <prefix>"
to pick one, then every following line is delivered to them as a chat
message. The special line
.B clip
shares the local clipboard with the selected peer - it becomes the clipboard
on the receiving device (Android or another
.BR up ).
Incoming messages print as
.BR "# 💬 <peer>: <text>" .
.SH OPTIONS
.B \-\-relay <url>
is repeatable on most commands and overrides the default relays.
.B \-\-identity
overrides the default shared identity file
(~/.noscat/identity.hex, auto-created on first use).
.B \-\-mesh [<dir>]
scopes the tunnel to a mesh (MLS membership becomes the authorization);
the value is optional and defaults to ~/.noscat/device.
.B \-\-name <name>
publishes a friendly device name with the advert (up defaults to the
hostname).
.B \-\-listen <addr>
binds UDP listeners. Use
.B \-\-help
on any command for the full list.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (for transfers: the whole file was received, flushed, and verified).
.TP
.B nonzero
Failure (timeouts, path loss, bad identities, refused transfers).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Server:
.PP
.nf
    noscat serve
.fi
.PP
Client:
.PP
.nf
    echo hello | noscat pipe <server's npub>
.fi
.PP
The node (scan its QR with a peer to learn its npub):
.PP
.nf
    noscat mesh create
    noscat up \-\-mesh
    noscat up \-\-mesh \-\-out ~/incoming \-\-to 127.0.0.1:22
.fi
.PP
Mesh-scoped file transfer:
.PP
.nf
    noscat receive \-\-out ./incoming \-\-mesh
    noscat send ./report.pdf <A's npub> \-\-mesh
.fi
